nt-black text-gray-900">Why</th></tr></thead><tbody class=3D"divide-y divid=
e-gray-100"><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Photo, hero image, bl=
og image</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-green-700">WebP</td>=
<td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Best balance of quality, size, and support=
</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">High-traffic landing pa=
ge hero</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-blue-700">AVIF</td><t=
d class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Extra compression savings justify encode tim=
e</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Logo, icon, UI screens=
hot</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-orange-700">PNG or SVG</t=
d><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Hard edges artifact badly under lossy co=
mpression</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Transparent ov=
erlay or cutout</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-green-700">We=
bP</td><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Alpha channel support with lossy co=
mpression</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Animated image=
</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-green-700">WebP or AVIF</td>=
<td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Far smaller than GIF; use video if longer =
than 3s</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Vector illustrat=
ion, icon, diagram</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-purple-700=
">SVG</td><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Resolution-independent, scales t=
o any size</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=3D"quality-settings" class=
=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-4">Qualit=
y Settings That Actually Make Sense</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 l=
eading-relaxed mb-4">Quality settings in lossy formats (WebP, JPEG, AVIF) w=
ork on a 0=E2=80=93100 scale, but the useful range is much narrower than th=
at. Below ~60, compression artifacts become visible. Above ~90, file sizes =
climb steeply with no perceptible quality gain.</p><p class=3D"text-lg text=
-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical ranges by use case:</p><div c=
lass=3D"overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table class=3D"w-full text-sm border-collaps=
e"><thead><tr class=3D"border-b-2 border-gray-200"><th class=3D"text-left p=
y-3 pr-6 font-black text-gray-900">Use Case</th><th class=3D"text-left py-3=
 pr-6 font-black text-gray-900">WebP Quality</th><th class=3D"text-left py-=
3 font-black text-gray-900">Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody class=3D"divide-y=
 divide-gray-100"><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">General web ima=
ges</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-gray-900">80=E2=80=9385</=
td><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Indistinguishable from source at normal=
 viewing</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Hero / full-ble=
ed images</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-gray-900">85=E2=80=
=9390</td><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Larger display size warrants sli=
ghtly higher quality</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Thu=
mbnails</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-gray-900">70=E2=80=93=
80</td><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Small display size hides compressio=
n well</td></tr><tr><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 text-gray-700">Product images (e=
-commerce)</td><td class=3D"py-3 pr-6 font-semibold text-gray-900">85=E2=80=
=9390</td><td class=3D"py-3 text-gray-600">Quality affects perceived produc=
t value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 le=
ading-relaxed mb-4">85 is a safe default for almost everything. The differe=
nce between 85 and 95 is rarely visible; the difference in file size is sig=
nificant.</p></div></div></section><section class=3D"w-full bg-white pt-24"=
><div class=3D"w-full px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8"><div class=3D"max-w-4xl mx-auto=
"><h2 id=3D"dimensions-and-responsive-images" class=3D"font-times text-4xl =
md:text-5xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tighter mb-6">Dimensions and =
Responsive Images</h2><p class=3D"text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-=
8">Format and quality handle compression. Dimensions handle the other half =
of the problem=E2=80=94serving the right number of pixels to begin with.</p=
><h3 id=3D"dimensions-problem" class=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-=
gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">The Dimensions Problem</h3><p class=3D"text-l=
g text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">A 4000=C3=973000px image displayed at=
 800=C3=97600px on screen has 25 times more pixels than the browser will ev=
er render. Every one of those extra pixels was downloaded, decoded, and thr=
own away. The browser cannot optimize this for you=E2=80=94it downloads the=
 full file before it knows the display size.</p><p class=3D"text-lg text-gr=
ay-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">Resize images to the largest size they'll be d=
isplayed at before uploading. For a blog post with a max content width of 8=
00px, your images should be 800px wide=E2=80=94not 1600px, not original cam=
era resolution. For retina displays (2x), 1600px makes sense. Going beyond =
that for a fixed-width content column doesn't.</p><h3 id=3D"srcset" class=
=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">srcset=
: Letting the Browser Choose</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-=
relaxed mb-4">For responsive layouts where the same image slot renders at d=
ifferent widths on different devices, <code class=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.=
5 rounded text-sm">srcset</code> lets you provide multiple image sizes and =
let the browser pick the most appropriate one based on the viewport and dev=
ice pixel ratio.</p><pre class=3D"bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 =
text-sm overflow-x-auto mb-6 leading-relaxed">&lt;img
  src=3D"photo-800.webp"
  srcset=3D"
    photo-400.webp  400w,
    photo-800.webp  800w,
    photo-1200.webp 1200w
  "
  sizes=3D"(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 800px"
  alt=3D"[Description of image]"
  loading=3D"lazy"
  width=3D"800"
  height=3D"600"
/&gt;</pre><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The <cod=
e class=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">sizes</code> attribute =
tells the browser how wide the image will actually be displayed=E2=80=94not=
 the viewport width. A browser on a 1200px viewport loading an image inside=
 an 800px content column should know it only needs the 800px file, not the =
1200px one.</p><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">Alwa=
ys include explicit <code class=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm"=
>width</code> and <code class=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">h=
eight</code> attributes. Without them, the browser doesn't know the aspect =
ratio until the image loads, which causes layout shift=E2=80=94a direct hit=
 to your Cumulative Layout Shift score.</p><h3 id=3D"picture-element" class=
=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">The pi=
cture Element for Format Fallbacks</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 le=
ading-relaxed mb-4">If you need to serve AVIF to browsers that support it a=
nd fall back to WebP for those that don't, use <code class=3D"bg-gray-100 p=
x-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">&lt;picture&gt;</code>:</p><pre class=3D"bg-gra=
y-900 text-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 text-sm overflow-x-auto mb-8 leading-rel=
axed">&lt;picture&gt;
  &lt;source srcset=3D"photo.avif" type=3D"image/avif" /&gt;
  &lt;source srcset=3D"photo.webp" type=3D"image/webp" /&gt;
  &lt;img src=3D"photo.jpg" alt=3D"[Description of image]" loading=3D"lazy"=
 width=3D"800" height=3D"600" /&gt;
&lt;/picture&gt;</pre><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-=
4">The browser uses the first <code class=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounde=
d text-sm">&lt;source&gt;</code> it supports and falls back to the <code cl=
ass=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">&lt;img&gt;</code> tag. JPE=
G as the final fallback means even IE11 gets something=E2=80=94not that you=
 probably care, but it costs nothing.</p></div></div></section><section cla=
ss=3D"w-full bg-white pt-24"><div class=3D"w-full px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8"><di=
v class=3D"max-w-4xl mx-auto"><h2 id=3D"loading-metadata-alt-text" class=3D=
"font-times text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tighter =
mb-6">Loading Behavior, Metadata, and Alt Text</h2><h3 id=3D"lazy-loading" =
class=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">l=
oading=3D"lazy" and loading=3D"eager"</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700=
 leading-relaxed mb-4"><code class=3D"bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-=
sm">loading=3D"lazy"</code> defers image download until the image is close =
to the viewport. It's a one-attribute change that can cut initial page load=
 time significantly on image-heavy pages. Add it to every image that isn't =
visible on first load.</p><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed=
 mb-8">The exception is above-the-fold images=E2=80=94your hero, your logo,=
 anything visible before the user scrolls. Those should be <code class=3D"b=
g-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">loading=3D"eager"</code> (or no att=
ribute at all, since eager is the default). Lazy-loading above-the-fold ima=
ges delays the Largest Contentful Paint and actively hurts your score.</p><=
h3 id=3D"fetchpriority" class=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-90=
0 tracking-tight mb-3">fetchpriority=3D"high"</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-=
gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">For your single most important above-the-fol=
d image=E2=80=94typically the hero=E2=80=94add <code class=3D"bg-gray-100 p=
x-1 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">fetchpriority=3D"high"</code>. This hints to th=
e browser that this image should be prioritized in the network queue. It's =
particularly effective when the image is your LCP element. Only use it on o=
ne image per page; applying it broadly defeats the purpose.</p><h3 id=3D"st=
rip-metadata" class=3D"font-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 tracking=
-tight mb-3">Strip Metadata</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-r=
elaxed mb-8">Camera images contain EXIF metadata=E2=80=94GPS coordinates, c=
amera model, shooting settings, timestamps. This data is invisible to visit=
ors but adds kilobytes to every file. XnConvert strips it by default when c=
onverting. If you're processing images another way, make sure metadata stri=
pping is enabled. There's no reason to serve a visitor the GPS coordinates =
of where you took a photo.</p><h3 id=3D"alt-text-and-file-names" class=3D"f=
ont-times text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">Alt Text an=
d File Names</h3><p class=3D"text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Al=
t text serves two purposes: accessibility (screen readers read it aloud) an=
d SEO (it's the primary signal Google uses to understand image content). It=
 should describe what's in the image specifically=E2=80=94not keyword-stuff=
ed, not empty, not "image of."</p><div class=3D"bg-gray-50 border border-gr=
ay-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-4"><p class=3D"text-sm font-bold text-red-600 mb-2=
